<section class="section">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-head center reveal">
      <div class="eyebrow" style="color:var(--gold)">Two ways to fund a flip</div>
      <h2>Most Lenders Want 10–20% Down. We Have a Zero-Cash Path.</h2>
      <p>Every fix-and-flip lender on your shortlist funds a percentage of cost and asks you to bring the rest. RECR does that too — and, on deals that qualify, an alternative that requires none of your cash. We start with the deal and tell you which path fits.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="program-band reveal">
      <div class="program-panel">
        <span class="tag">Path one · Conventional flip debt</span>
        <h3>Leverage on Cost, You Bring the Rest</h3>
        <p>The standard structure: capital covers most of the purchase and the rehab in draws; you contribute the balance and the buy-side costs. Fastest to set up, and you keep all of the resale profit.</p>
        <ul class="list-check">
          <li>Purchase to <span class="pending">{{CLAIM:fixflip.max_ltc}}, confirming</span>, rehab funded in draws</li>
          <li>You bring the down payment and closing costs</li>
          <li>You keep 100% of the resale profit</li>
          <li>Best when you have cash and want all the upside</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="program-panel premium">
        <span class="tag">Path two · 100% joint venture</span>
        <h3>$0 Down on Qualifying Deals</h3>
        <p>When total project cost lands within 70% of a defensible ARV, the joint-venture structure can fund <strong>100% of purchase, rehab, and closing</strong> — no cash from you. The capital partner shares project economics instead of charging a down payment.</p>
        <ul class="list-check">
          <li>Full stack covered — purchase, rehab, and closing</li>
          <li>The gate: total project cost within 70% of ARV</li>
          <li>Profit split per the joint-venture agreement</li>
          <li>Best when you have the deal, not the cash</li>
        </ul>
        <a class="btn btn-white" href="/purchase-rehab/" style="margin-top:1rem">See the 100% JV Path →</a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="section-note reveal">Up to 100% applies to qualifying joint-venture transactions within 70% of ARV only. Subject to ARV support, documentation, total project economics, program availability, underwriting, and final approval. Not all transactions qualify.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--gray">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="two-col">
      <div class="reveal-left">
        <h2 style="font-size:1.6rem;letter-spacing:-0.02em">Who Fix and Flip Loans Are For</h2>
        <p>A fix and flip loan is built for an operator who can find a property below market, has a credible plan to renovate it, and intends to exit within months rather than hold it for years.</p>
        <ul class="list-check">
          <li>Investors buying distressed or dated houses at a genuine discount to resale value</li>
          <li>Operators who want to keep their own cash liquid for contingency or the next deal</li>
          <li>Flippers running more than one project who need capital that scales with deal flow</li>
          <li>BRRRR investors who will renovate now and refinance into a rental at stabilization</li>
          <li>Newer operators with a cosmetic-to-moderate scope and a defensible ARV spread</li>
        </ul>
        <h2 style="font-size:1.6rem;letter-spacing:-0.02em;margin-top:2rem">Documentation</h2>
        <ul class="list-check">
          <li><strong>Purchase contract or LOI</strong> — the real basis, not an asking price</li>
          <li><strong>Line-item rehab budget by trade</strong> — a lump sum will not underwrite</li>
          <li><strong>Comparable sales supporting ARV</strong> — the number the whole deal rests on</li>
          <li><strong>Photos or an inspection</strong> — confirms the scope matches the budget</li>
          <li><strong>Scope of work and timeline</strong> — what gets done, in what order, by when</li>
          <li><strong>Experience summary and entity documents</strong> — prior projects and the borrowing entity</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="reveal-right">
        <h2 style="font-size:1.6rem;letter-spacing:-0.02em">How a Flip Is Evaluated</h2>
        <p>Because a fix and flip is repaid from the resale, the review concentrates on whether the finished property can carry the debt with room to spare.</p>
        <ul class="list-check">
          <li><strong>After-repair value.</strong> Comparable sales an appraiser would stand behind, not optimistic pricing</li>
          <li><strong>Purchase discount.</strong> Whether you bought with enough spread beneath ARV to make the numbers work</li>
          <li><strong>Rehab budget realism.</strong> Line-item scope with contingency, matched to the condition of the house</li>
          <li><strong>Total cost against ARV.</strong> The screen that decides how much leverage a deal can safely carry</li>
          <li><strong>Exit credibility.</strong> A resale price the market supports, or a refinance that genuinely underwrites</li>
          <li><strong>Timeline.</strong> Whether the schedule is achievable for the work described, plus holding cost</li>
          <li><strong>Operator capability.</strong> Whether this person can execute this specific scope on budget</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="margin-top:1rem">Run the numbers before you submit. The <a href="/tools/deal-analyzer/" style="color:var(--blue);font-weight:600">deal analyzer</a> and the <a href="/tools/mao/" style="color:var(--blue);font-weight:600">maximum allowable offer calculator</a> build exactly the figures this review needs, and <a href="/resources/understanding-arv/" style="color:var(--blue);font-weight:600">how ARV is determined</a> walks through the value that anchors every flip.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

