<section class="section section--soft" id="the-math">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-head reveal">
      <div class="eyebrow" style="color:var(--gold)">The full math</div>
      <h2>The 70% Rule, Worked All the Way Through</h2>
      <p>The 70% rule is the screen flippers use to decide the most they should pay: purchase plus rehab should sit within 70% of a defensible ARV, leaving the rest for selling costs, holding, financing, and profit. Here it is on a real set of numbers.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="table-scroll reveal">
      <table class="worked">
        <caption>Illustrative only. Not a quote, not an offer, not a representation of terms available for any specific transaction.</caption>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">After-repair value (ARV)</th><td class="num">$300,000</td><td>Supported by comparable sales</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">70% of ARV</th><td class="num">$210,000</td><td>The ceiling for purchase + rehab</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Less rehab budget</th><td class="num">−$45,000</td><td>Line-item scope by trade</td></tr>
          <tr class="total"><td>Maximum allowable offer (MAO)</td><td class="num">$165,000</td><td>The most the 70% rule says to pay</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Actual purchase price</th><td class="num">$160,000</td><td>Bought $5,000 under MAO</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Rehab budget</th><td class="num">$45,000</td><td>Released in draws</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Buy-side closing</th><td class="num">$4,000</td><td>Title, transfer, fees</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Holding costs (~5 mo)</th><td class="num">$8,000</td><td>Taxes, insurance, utilities, financing</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Sell-side costs (8%)</th><td class="num">$24,000</td><td>Commission, closing, concessions</td></tr>
          <tr class="total"><td>Total project cost</td><td class="num">$241,000</td><td>Everything in and out</td></tr>
          <tr class="total"><td>Gross project profit</td><td class="num">$59,000</td><td>Resale $300,000 less total cost</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <h3 style="margin-top:1.5rem;color:var(--navy)">Reading the example</h3>
    <p>Purchase plus rehab is $205,000 — that lands at roughly <strong>68% of the $300,000 ARV</strong>, comfortably under the 70% ceiling. That margin is not decoration. It is what pays the selling costs, the holding costs, and the profit, and it is the cushion that protects the loan if the house sells for $285,000 instead of $300,000. Push the same purchase and rehab against a $270,000 ARV and the ratio climbs past 75%, the cushion evaporates, and a single soft comp or a two-month-longer hold turns the profit negative.</p>
    <p><strong>This is the screen, not a promise.</strong> Clearing the 70% rule tells you a deal is worth underwriting; it does not set your leverage. How much of the $241,000 stack the capital covers, at what rate and term, is confirmed per transaction — <span class="pending">up to {{CLAIM:fixflip.max_ltc}} of cost, confirming against the current program sheet</span>. If the deal is strong enough to land total project cost within 70% of ARV, the <a href="/purchase-rehab/" style="color:var(--blue);font-weight:600">100% purchase and rehab joint-venture path</a> can cover the entire stack with no cash from you — a different structure for the same house. Build your own numbers first with the <a href="/tools/mao/" style="color:var(--blue);font-weight:600">maximum allowable offer calculator</a>.</p>
    <div class="hero-actions" style="margin-top:1.25rem"><a class="btn btn-primary" href="/submit-a-deal/">Submit a Deal for Review</a></div>
  </div>
</section>

