<section class="section section--navy">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="two-col">
      <div class="reveal-left">
        <h2 style="color:#fff;font-size:1.6rem">Step-by-Step Process</h2>
        <div class="steps" style="grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:.75rem;margin-top:1rem">
          <div class="step"><h3>Preliminary review</h3><p>Send the address, purchase price, rehab budget, and your ARV support. Enough for a first read on whether the flip pencils — same day on a complete submission.</p></div>
          <div class="step"><h3>ARV and cost screen</h3><p>We test purchase plus rehab against a defensible ARV. Most deals are confirmed or redirected here — fast, so you are not waiting on a no.</p></div>
          <div class="step"><h3>Same-day approval</h3><p>If the economics support it, you get a credit decision or term sheet the same day, covering leverage, the draw schedule, the term, and the takeout.</p></div>
          <div class="step"><h3>Full file and underwriting</h3><p>Documentation, valuation, and budget review move through the applicable process with the capital resource. Title and appraisal timing sit outside our control.</p></div>
          <div class="step"><h3>Close and fund in 3–5 days</h3><p>From an approved, complete file, funding runs 3–5 business days. Acquisition funds at closing; rehab releases in draws against verified completed work.</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="reveal-right">
        <h2 style="color:#fff;font-size:1.6rem">When a Flip Does Not Fit</h2>
        <p class="muted">We would rather tell you in the first conversation than after three weeks of document collection.</p>
        <ul class="list-check" style="margin-top:1rem">
          <li style="color:rgba(255,255,255,.9)"><strong style="color:#fff">ARV not supported by comps.</strong> The most common reason a flip is declined — the exit price is aspirational, not evidenced</li>
          <li style="color:rgba(255,255,255,.9)"><strong style="color:#fff">Purchase and rehab above 70% of ARV.</strong> There is not enough room beneath value to carry costs and risk</li>
          <li style="color:rgba(255,255,255,.9)"><strong style="color:#fff">No contingency in the budget.</strong> A rehab number with no cushion has not been thought through</li>
          <li style="color:rgba(255,255,255,.9)"><strong style="color:#fff">Scope beyond demonstrated experience.</strong> A first full gut with no track record is a hard conversation</li>
          <li style="color:rgba(255,255,255,.9)"><strong style="color:#fff">No credible exit.</strong> "I'll list it and see" is not a resale plan, and a refinance has to actually underwrite</li>
          <li style="color:rgba(255,255,255,.9)"><strong style="color:#fff">Thin margin, long timeline.</strong> A small spread over many months is where holding cost quietly consumes the profit</li>
          <li style="color:rgba(255,255,255,.9)"><strong style="color:#fff">Owner-occupied intent.</strong> Business-purpose, non-owner-occupied transactions only</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="color:rgba(255,255,255,.82);margin-top:1rem">If a flip is not the right structure, that does not end the conversation. A declined flip may still fit a <a href="/rental/" style="color:var(--gold);font-weight:600">rental hold</a> or the <a href="/purchase-rehab/" style="color:var(--gold);font-weight:600">100% JV path</a>. <a href="/loan-products/" style="color:var(--gold);font-weight:600">Compare all programs</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-head center reveal"><div class="eyebrow" style="color:var(--gold)">FAQ</div><h2>Questions About Fix and Flip Loans</h2></div>
    <div class="faq-list reveal">
      <details class="faq-item"><summary>What is a fix and flip loan?</summary><div class="faq-body"><p>Short-term capital that funds the purchase and renovation of a distressed property you intend to resell or refinance. It is underwritten on the project — the discount at purchase, the rehab budget, and the after-repair value — rather than primarily on your income, and the rehab portion typically releases in draws as work is completed.</p></div></details>
      <details class="faq-item"><summary>How much of the deal will a fix and flip loan cover?</summary><div class="faq-body"><p>Leverage is set per transaction and by the capital resource carrying the file — <span class="pending">up to {{CLAIM:fixflip.max_ltc}} of cost and {{CLAIM:fixflip.max_arv}} against ARV, confirming against the current program sheet</span>. Deals that land total project cost within 70% of ARV may instead qualify for the <a href="/purchase-rehab/" style="color:var(--blue);font-weight:600">100% purchase and rehab</a> joint-venture structure with no cash from you.</p></div></details>
      <details class="faq-item"><summary>What is the 70% rule?</summary><div class="faq-body"><p>A screening heuristic flippers use: the most you should pay for a property is 70% of its after-repair value minus the rehab budget. It keeps purchase plus rehab within roughly 70% of ARV, leaving the remaining margin for selling costs, holding costs, financing, and profit. It tells you a deal is worth underwriting — it does not set your leverage.</p></div></details>
      <details class="faq-item"><summary>How is the rehab budget released?</summary><div class="faq-body"><p>In draws, against verified completed work. You request a draw, the completed stage is confirmed, and funds release for that portion of the scope. The purchase funds at closing; the rehab does not arrive as a lump sum you hold.</p></div></details>
      <details class="faq-item"><summary>What credit score do I need?</summary><div class="faq-body"><p>Credit is one input weighed alongside the deal economics, your experience, and the exit — not the single gate it would be in conventional lending. The specific threshold is confirmed per transaction: <span class="pending">{{CLAIM:fixflip.min_credit}}, confirming</span>.</p></div></details>
      <details class="faq-item"><summary>Can a first-time flipper get funded?</summary><div class="faq-body"><p>Yes, depending on scope. A first-timer with a cosmetic rehab, a strong ARV spread, and a realistic budget is a more straightforward file than an experienced operator with a thin gut. Experience is a risk factor, not an automatic disqualifier. See <a href="/first-time/" style="color:var(--blue);font-weight:600">first-time investors</a>.</p></div></details>
      <details class="faq-item"><summary>What loan sizes and terms are available?</summary><div class="faq-body"><p>Loan size and term are set per transaction — <span class="pending">{{CLAIM:fixflip.loan_size_range}} over {{CLAIM:fixflip.term_range}}, confirming against the current program sheet</span>. A flip term is built around the renovation schedule plus a realistic sale window, with the exit defined up front.</p></div></details>
      <details class="faq-item"><summary>What rate and points should I expect?</summary><div class="faq-body"><p>Pricing depends on the profile, the leverage, and the capital resource — <span class="pending">{{CLAIM:fixflip.rate_range}}, confirming</span>. Because a flip is short-term, the holding-cost impact of the rate is a function of how many months you carry the property, which is why the timeline gets as much scrutiny as the rate.</p></div></details>
      <details class="faq-item"><summary>How does BRRRR work with a fix and flip loan?</summary><div class="faq-body"><p>Buy, rehabilitate, rent, refinance, repeat. The fix and flip loan funds the renovation, then the exit is a <a href="/rental/" style="color:var(--blue);font-weight:600">rental / DSCR refinance</a> rather than a sale. The stabilized rent has to support the takeout, so both the rehab math and the finished cash-flow matter.</p></div></details>
      <details class="faq-item"><summary>How fast can a flip close?</summary><div class="faq-body"><p><strong>Same-day approval on a complete submission, and funding in 3–5 business days from an approved file.</strong> The word "complete" is doing real work in that sentence: we need the address, purchase price, a line-item rehab budget, ARV support, and your entity basics. A file missing the ARV comps or carrying a lump-sum rehab number cannot be approved same day, because there is nothing to approve against. Valuation and title also sit outside our control, so they set the floor on the funding side.</p></div></details>
      <details class="faq-item"><summary>Is "same-day approval" the same as same-day money?</summary><div class="faq-body"><p>No, and anyone blurring the two is selling you something. Approval is a credit decision or term sheet — confirmation that the deal works and on what structure. Funding is money at the closing table, which runs 3–5 business days behind it and depends on valuation, title, and third-party reports. We separate the two deliberately so you can plan a contract deadline around the number that actually matters to you.</p></div></details>
      <details class="faq-item"><summary>What happens if the flip does not sell?</summary><div class="faq-body"><p>This is why the exit and a stress case are underwritten up front. A common fallback is refinancing into a rental hold if the resale market softens, which is one reason a deal that also cash-flows as a rental is a stronger file. The specifics live in the loan documents and vary by transaction.</p></div></details>
      <details class="faq-item"><summary>How much money do I need to start flipping houses?</summary><div class="faq-body"><p>On conventional flip debt, plan for a down payment on cost plus buy-side closing costs and a reserve for overruns — the exact contribution is confirmed per transaction (<span class="pending">{{CLAIM:fixflip.max_ltc}}, confirming</span>). On a qualifying joint-venture deal where total project cost lands within 70% of a defensible ARV, the <a href="/purchase-rehab/" style="color:var(--blue);font-weight:600">100% purchase and rehab structure</a> can cover purchase, rehab, and closing with no cash from you. Reserves are still strongly preferred either way, because projects encounter surprises.</p></div></details>
      <details class="faq-item"><summary>Do I need a down payment?</summary><div class="faq-body"><p>For a conventional fix and flip loan, yes — capital funds a percentage of cost and you bring the balance. For a qualifying joint-venture transaction within 70% of ARV, no: the structure covers 100% of purchase, rehab, and closing, and the capital partner shares project economics instead of taking a down payment. Which path fits is decided by the deal, not by your preference.</p></div></details>
      <details class="faq-item"><summary>Is a fix and flip loan the same as hard money?</summary><div class="faq-body"><p>They overlap. "Hard money" describes asset-based short-term capital secured by property; a fix and flip loan is that capital applied to a buy-renovate-resell project, with the renovation funded in draws. The meaningful difference is not the label but whether the file is underwritten on a defensible ARV and a credible exit, or simply on the collateral.</p></div></details>
      <details class="faq-item"><summary>Can I fund more than one flip at a time?</summary><div class="faq-body"><p>Yes — each project is underwritten on its own merits, and running concurrent deals is common for operators with the capacity to manage them. What tightens as deal count rises is scrutiny of your bandwidth, your reserves, and the performance of projects already in progress. See <a href="/experienced/" style="color:var(--blue);font-weight:600">experienced investors</a>.</p></div></details>
      <details class="faq-item"><summary>Does submitting a deal guarantee funding?</summary><div class="faq-body"><p>No. Submission begins a review. All financing is subject to program availability, underwriting, property eligibility, borrower qualification, and final approval.</p></div></details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

